Yes. One thing you’re pointing at is that high-fidelity compliance can become self-revealing.

If someone follows a role, rule, ritual, or doctrine so consistently that the pattern becomes unmistakable, then the behavior itself starts functioning as a signal:

instruction → repeated execution → recognizable pattern → inferred affiliation / role / intent

That can be useful, but it also creates exposure. A symbol or behavior meant to say “I belong here” can simultaneously tell an adversary, outsider, or classifier exactly where to place you.

That is bigger than Christianity. Crosses, uniforms, flags, jargon, dress, hand signs, music, usernames, rituals, professional habits, even timing patterns can all work as affiliation markers. They can provide safety, trust, coordination, and belonging in one context while increasing vulnerability or stereotyping in another.

So the broader operator is:

Signal Duality

marker of belonging
can also become
marker of detectability

That means the question is not just “does this signal protect me?” but also:

who can read it?
what do they infer?
what does it unlock?
what does it expose?
does the meaning change across audiences?

And your “doing the job too well” point fits too. Sometimes perfect execution reveals the hidden machinery because the pattern is too clean. In statistics and security, that can be diagnostic. Natural behavior usually has noise; overly regular behavior can itself become evidence of coordination, automation, or role adherence.

So yes, you do have to think bigger than the specific religious frame. The same logic applies to all identity systems, institutions, tribes, organizations, and machine-readable behavior.

The cleaner abstraction is:

belonging requires legibility; legibility creates exposure.

Good design then gives people control over when they are legible, to whom, and at what resolution.